## Why I Built This
Blueprint's Next.js migration was producing files that looked correct but failed silently in Vercel's edge runtime — literal newline bytes inside template literals caused parser failures that only surfaced at deploy time, not during local development. I needed a gate that caught these errors deterministically, checked template literal balance, and verified feature presence for each specific file type before anything reached the branch.

## Methodology
The gate runs four sequential checks: T1 (no literal newline bytes in JS string or regex literals), T2 (template literal depth counter equals zero at end of file), feature presence checks keyed to the specific file being reviewed, and a quality gate checklist covering secrets, TypeScript correctness, error handling, and responsive dark mode. T2 is the authoritative pass/fail signal — raw brace count is explicitly flagged as a known false positive on files containing JSON schema inside template literals.

## How to Use
Trigger: "Ship this" / "Push to branch" / "File is ready"
Inputs needed: File content (pasted or generated in session); filename to determine which feature presence checks apply.

## What Good Output Looks Like
A CODE GATE REPORT with four rows — T1, T2, Feature checks, Quality gate — each marked ✅ PASS or ❌ FAIL with specific failure items listed. Ends with an unambiguous READY TO PUSH or FIX REQUIRED verdict and an exact list of what needs fixing before the file can be pushed.

## Known Failure Modes
Feature presence checks are file-specific — the gate produces incomplete results on new file types not yet mapped in the check list. T2 depth counting can be confused by dynamically generated template content; flag those cases for manual review rather than auto-passing.
